00:00:11.156 --> 00:00:14.516
Hi everyone. Welcome back to our session at track four.

00:00:14.796 --> 00:00:21.116
So I'm Yunyun and today with me we have Jonathan who is from
Astrata Group and I'm from AWS.

00:00:21.316 --> 00:00:27.956
So it's very nice to have all of you seated here ready
to listen to how quick can help you with your business.

00:00:28.356 --> 00:00:31.436
So just a quick run through of the agenda for today.

00:00:31.796 --> 00:00:38.956
So first we are going to introduce what Amazon Quick is followed
by two of the automated agents feature within it

00:00:38.956 --> 00:00:41.796
which is Quick flows and Quick Automate.

00:00:42.076 --> 00:00:45.836
Then we also have a customer case study with Astrata Group,

00:00:46.156 --> 00:00:53.236
who will be sharing a real life story of how they have managed
to use quick to innovate their company's processes.

00:00:53.476 --> 00:00:58.956
Finally, we will wrap up on the next steps so
that you can get your hands dirty and try out quick.

00:00:59.396 --> 00:01:02.756
So without further ado, let me just start on quick.

00:01:03.316 --> 00:01:09.596
So at AWS, we always start from working
backwards from all of you who are our customers.

00:01:09.756 --> 00:01:15.236
So we want to understand what problems you're trying to solve,
what challenges you need help with.

00:01:15.396 --> 00:01:23.916
And many of you in this room have already shared your expectation
of how you want to use AI, what you're hoping to get out of it,

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:28.596
and how you're thinking about
AI agents as to where they fit in your businesses.

00:01:29.196 --> 00:01:32.276
So according to some of the statistics that we have gathered,

00:01:32.276 --> 00:01:41.316
we see that 82% of organizations believe that they'll integrate
AI agents into their business process within 1 to 3 years.

00:01:41.716 --> 00:01:49.596
But in that same window, only 15% of the business
decisions are expected to be made by those agents.

00:01:50.156 --> 00:01:57.636
The gap is telling. So if agents are embedded
everywhere but only driving 15% of our decisions.

00:01:57.876 --> 00:02:03.236
What does that mean? That means
that humans are still ultimately handling the rest.

00:02:03.676 --> 00:02:06.476
And we are often stuck doing repetitive work.

00:02:06.836 --> 00:02:09.316
And let's just say that's not transformation.

00:02:09.556 --> 00:02:12.596
That's just a new layer on top of the old way.

00:02:12.636 --> 00:02:20.476
We have been doing things. And when we look at these numbers,
the question that we at AWS kept coming back to was,

00:02:20.676 --> 00:02:26.236
how do we help employees work with AI and agent more effectively?

00:02:26.836 --> 00:02:30.396
And automation is a big part of that answer.

00:02:30.796 --> 00:02:34.036
And it's exactly where it sits at the heart of quick.

00:02:36.476 --> 00:02:40.476
And automation isn't just about your back office processes.

00:02:40.676 --> 00:02:45.196
It's about how business users work, how knowledge workers work.

00:02:45.636 --> 00:02:49.156
In short, how most of us work every single day.

00:02:49.956 --> 00:02:52.356
Think about how your typical day starts.

00:02:52.716 --> 00:02:54.876
There's a problem for you to solve at work.

00:02:55.196 --> 00:03:01.196
A decision to make and something
to improve for your business or your own customers.

00:03:01.716 --> 00:03:06.356
And that exact process generally breaks down into three pillars.

00:03:06.996 --> 00:03:14.396
So first, you are gathering information from internal systems,
external sources, doing your own research.

00:03:14.796 --> 00:03:21.876
With that, you need to make sense of it,
which often means looping in a data analyst, a researcher,

00:03:21.876 --> 00:03:25.236
or a bi engineer to get the dashboard built.

00:03:25.916 --> 00:03:31.636
By the time all of that comes together,
a significant amount of time has already passed.

00:03:31.836 --> 00:03:35.876
And guess what? You haven't even made a decision yet.

00:03:36.636 --> 00:03:40.076
And that's where the human expertise really matters.

00:03:40.436 --> 00:03:46.436
You take all the data, apply your judgment,
and drive towards a better outcome.

00:03:47.196 --> 00:03:49.316
But it's a heavy lift, I understand.

00:03:49.716 --> 00:03:53.636
And because of that, the very next day you go back to work.

00:03:53.916 --> 00:03:57.476
You do a very similar version of it all over again.

00:03:58.276 --> 00:04:06.716
But don't get me wrong. That cycle, which is valuable yet exhausting,
is exactly what quick is designed to help with.

00:04:07.436 --> 00:04:13.596
The goal is to let agents handle
the heavy lifting of gathering, analyzing,

00:04:13.596 --> 00:04:19.676
and surfacing information so business
users can focus on what they do best,

00:04:20.036 --> 00:04:25.316
which is to make good business
decisions to drive the business forward.

00:04:27.316 --> 00:04:33.036
Quick is fundamentally a new way
that you can work in the AI enterprise.

00:04:33.556 --> 00:04:43.516
At its core, it's a unified workplace where humans and AI agents
collaborate over a secure, connected view of all your data.

00:04:44.356 --> 00:04:53.076
And when we say all your data, we mean it going from
structured data from Aurora Redshift your data lakes

00:04:53.076 --> 00:04:57.836
or data warehouses, even if they don't reside in AWS.

00:04:58.276 --> 00:05:00.000
Together with unstructured data from S3,

